{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "import math\n\ndef closest_integer(value):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the closest integer to a given numeric string,\n    rounding equidistant values away from zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    num_float = float(value)\n\n    # Determine the sign for the final result.\n    sign = 1 if num_float >= 0 else -1\n\n    # Work with the absolute value to simplify rounding logic.\n    abs_val = abs(num_float)\n\n    # Calculate the fractional part of the absolute value.\n    fractional_part = abs_val - math.floor(abs_val)\n\n    if fractional_part > 0.5:\n        # If fractional part is greater than 0.5, round up (e.g., 15.7 rounds to 16).\n        return int(math.ceil(abs_val) * sign)\n    elif fractional_part < 0.5:\n        # If fractional part is less than 0.5, round down (e.g., 15.3 rounds to 15).\n        return int(math.floor(abs_val) * sign)\n    else: # fractional_part == 0.5\n        # If fractional part is exactly 0.5, round away from zero.\n        # math.ceil(abs_val) for 14.5 is 15.0; applying sign (1) gives 15.\n        # math.ceil(abs_val) for -14.5 (abs 14.5) is 15.0; applying sign (-1) gives -15.\n        return int(math.ceil(abs_val) * sign)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "def closest_integer(value):\n    '''\n    Create a function that takes a value (string) representing a number\n    and returns the closest integer to it. If the number is equidistant\n    from two integers, round it away from zero.\n    '''\n    num = float(value)\n    if num >= 0:\n        floor_num = int(num)\n        if num - floor_num >= 0.5:\n            return floor_num + 1\n        else:\n            return floor_num\n    else:\n        ceil_num = int(num)\n        if num - ceil_num <= -0.5:\n            return ceil_num - 1\n        else:\n            return ceil_num"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/100", "solution": "def make_a_pile(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, you have to make a pile of n levels of stones.\n    The first level has n stones.\n    The number of stones in the next level is:\n        - the next odd number if n is odd.\n        - the next even number if n is even.\n    Return the number of stones in each level in a list, where element at index\n    i represents the number of stones in the level (i+1).\n    \"\"\"\n    pile = []\n    current_stones = n\n    for _ in range(n):\n        pile.append(current_stones)\n        current_stones += 2\n    return pile"}
